      "functional_impact_measured": "Surface expression assay (LgBiT complementation): HiBiT-E-selectin showed robust ligand-induced surface upregulation in response to six stimuli, confirming functional trafficking and surface display; no loss of inducibility reported.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "All three ledger lines describe the same published insertion: an 11 aa HiBiT tag placed at the N-terminus of endogenous E-selectin via CRISPR-Cas9 in HUVECs. The paper states 'N terminus' but gives no residue number, so the position is topology-derived. E-selectin (UniProt P16581) is a type I single-pass transmembrane protein: the signal peptide spans residues 1\u201321 (authoritative landmark), the ectodomain spans 22\u2013556, the TM spans 557\u2013577, and the cytoplasmic tail spans 578\u2013610. The C-terminus is therefore intracellular. The mature N-terminus (residue 22, W) is the sole extracellular terminus, making it the only possible location for an extracellular N-terminal tag. Python-verified: residue 21 = A (last of signal peptide), residue 22 = W (first of mature ectodomain); insert_after_residue = 21 places the tag between the cleaved signal peptide and the mature protein, consistent with standard signal-peptide-followed-by-tag architecture. Distance to TM boundary: 535 residues \u2014 no steric concern. The tag was expressed from the endogenous locus and showed normal ligand-induced surface trafficking, confirming functional tolerance. CRISPR-Cas9 knock-in of an 11 aa HiBiT tag at the N-terminus of endogenous E-selectin in primary HUVECs and immortalized TERT2-HUVECs. Cell-surface expression of the tagged protein was measured by membrane-impermeable LgBiT complementation (nanoluciferase). Ligand-induced surface expression was confirmed in response to TNF\u03b1, IL-1\u03b1, IL-1\u03b2, LPS, VEGF165a, and histamine. [validation: surface_and_function; position: topology_derived; source: paper; entailment_verified: True]",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 37496673,
          "citation": "PMID 37496673",
          "claim": "To ensure that ligand-induced changes were monitored in physiologically relevant cells under the control of their native promoters, we used CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing to attach the 11 amino acid HiBiT tag 18 , 19 to the N terminus of endogenous E-selectin expressed in both HUVECs and immortalized TERT2-HUVECs. 20 Measurement of cell surface expression of HiBiT-tagged E-selectin was then made following addition of exogenous membrane-impermeable LgBiT to achieve self-complementation of the full-length nanoluciferase enzyme."
        }
      ],
